## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a real-time, multi-tenant prompt routing system with integrated safety guards and dynamic policy management.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic policy store with hot-reload capabilities without redeployment
- PII/secrets detection and safe fallback mechanisms
- Latency budgeting and strategic caching
- Edge-case handling: conflicting signals, missing authentication

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal router prototype
const policy = { maxLatencyMs: 120, allowedTopics: ['query','analysis'] };
function route(prompt, userAuth) {
  const t0 = performance.now();

  // PII detection
  if (containsPII(prompt)) return { module: 'SafetyGuard', reason: 'PII detected' };

  // Policy validation
  if (!validatePolicy(prompt, policy)) return { module: 'ActionOrchestrator', reason: 'Policy constraint' };

  // Auth check
  if (!userAuth) return { module: 'SafetyGuard', reason: 'Missing authentication' };

  // Default routing
  return { module: 'PromptParser', reason: 'Standard query' };
}
```

## Implementation Considerations

- Redis for policy hot-reloads without service restart
- In-memory caching for frequently accessed policy rules
- Circuit breakers for policy store failures
- Metrics tracking for latency compliance

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design end-to-end prompt orchestration under SLA; covers multi-model choice, caching, and budget-based pruning in a multi-tenant context.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic plan generation with per-model latency and cost profiles
- Content-addressable caching to reduce repeated work
- Budget-aware pruning and graceful degradation
- Tenant isolation and guardrails in orchestration

## Code Example

```javascript
class MockModel {
  constructor(name, tokenCost, latencyMs){
    this.name = name; this.tokenCost = tokenCost; this.latencyMs = latencyMs;
  }
  run(prompt){
    return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve(`${this.name}-out:${prompt.slice(0,60)}`), this.latencyMs));
  }
}

const modelA = new MockModel('ModelA', 0.5, 120);
const modelB = new MockModel('ModelB', 0.2, 60);
const cache = new Map();
function hash(s){ let h=0; for(let i=0;i<s.length;i++){ h=(h<<5)-h+s.charCodeAt(i); h|=0;} return h.toString(); }
function plan(intent){ return [ [modelA, intent+" [segment1]"], [modelB, intent+" [segment2]"] ]; }
async function runPlan(intent, budgetTokens, maxLatencyMs=300){
  const key = hash(intent);
  if(cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);
  const steps = plan(intent);
  let totalLatency = 0; const outputs = [];
  for(const [m,p] of steps){ const out = await m.run(p); outputs.push(out); totalLatency += m.latencyMs; if(totalLatency>maxLatencyMs) break; }
  const result = outputs.join(' | ');
  cache.set(key, result);
  return result;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design modular prompt orchestration and a concrete lightweight prototype. Checks how candidates formalize routing signals and edge-case handling in real-world content workflows.

## Key Concepts

- Modular prompt architecture and routing signals
- Edge-case handling for content-critical prompts
- Lightweight Python prototype for quick validation
- Payload shaping for downstream components

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal routing prototype (JavaScript-like pseudocode)
function routePrompt(query) {
  const q = (query || '').toLowerCase();
  const module = (q.includes('source') || q.includes('earnings') || q.includes('did'))
    ? 'Source-Validator' 
    : q.includes('hallucination') ? 'Hallucination-Detector' : 'Answer-Formatter';
  return {
    module,
    plan: ['collect sources','assess credibility'],
    payload: { query, required_sources: 5, confidence_target: 'high' }
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the routing decisions across diverse prompts?
- What metrics would you track to improve module selection over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to enforce data policies in prompt processing, not just theory. It probes edge-case handling and testability.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt safety layering
- Data_sensitivity tagging
- Policy-driven guard rails and auditing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal guard example
function guardPrompt(prompt, policy){
  const intent = parseIntent(prompt);
  const data = tagSensitivity(prompt, policy);
  if(!allowed(data, policy)){ throw new Error('Policy violation'); }
  const guarded = applyGuard(prompt, policy);
  const check = `compliant:${Boolean(guarded)}`;
  return {guardedPrompt: guarded, check};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you model conflicting signals (privacy vs analytics) and resolve them?
- How would you test for prompt leakage across multi-hop prompts?

## Why This Is Asked
